    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d466">

                <pb facs="1124" n="1076" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v06p1/pg1076"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">CFM files, lot M 88, box 169, “Churchill–Eden
                    Visit”</note>
                <head>No. 466<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of a Meeting of Secretary of
                        State Dulles and Foreign Secretary Eden</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d466-f1"
                    /></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi><lb/>CEV
                        MC–1</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <date ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content"
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1954-06-25">June 25, 1954</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="participants" type="description">
                    <label>Participants:</label>
                    <item><list type="simple">
                            <head><hi rendition="#italic">United States</hi></head>
                            <item>The Secretary</item>
                            <item>Mr. Merchant</item>
                        </list></item>
                    <item><list type="simple">
                            <head><hi rendition="#italic">United Kingdom</hi></head>
                            <item>Mr. Anthony Eden</item>
                            <item>Sir Harold Caccia</item>
                            <item>Christopher Soames</item>
                        </list></item>
                </list>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>Subject:</label>
                    <item>Germany and E.D.C.</item>
                </list>
                <p>At noon today while waiting to join the President and Prime Minister, there was a
                    considerable discussion of the problem of Germany. The Secretary and Mr. Eden
                    agreed that their estimates coincided on the importance of taking positive
                    action in support of Adenauer if the French Assembly recessed this summer
                    without ratifying EDC. They were in general agreement that a way should be found
                    to separate the contractuals from the EDC, without prejudice to possible later
                    action by the French Assembly in approving EDC in the fall. This would require
                    some undertaking from Adenauer <pb facs="1125" n="1077" xml:id="pg1077"/> that
                    Germany would not unilaterally rearm during the period in which the fate of EDC
                    would be settled. The Secretary explained the constitutional requirements with
                    which we were faced in connection with modifying the Bonn Treaty so as to remove
                    the link with the EDC Treaty.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d466-f2"/> Mr. Eden was
                    obviously unaware of this complication but recognized it when it was pointed
                    out. There was agreement that Adenauer should be consulted before any approach
                    to the French.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d466-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d466-f1-content">
                <p>Drafted on June 27. According to the notation on the source text this meeting
                    took place at the White House at noon on June 26; however, the schedule of
                    meetings indicates that on June 26 Dulles and Eden were meeting at the
                    Department of State at noon. Presumably then, this meeting took place on June
                    25, immediately before that described in CEV MC–2, <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >infra</hi>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d466-f2" n="2" xml:id="d466-f2-content">
                <p>For the text of the Convention on Relations Between the Three Powers and the
                    Federal Republic of Germany (the Bonn Treaty), signed at Bonn on May 26, 1952,
                    see <ref target="frus1952-54v07p1#pg_112">vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >vii</hi>, Part 1, p. 112</ref>; for the text of the EDC Treaty, signed
                    at Paris on May 27, 1952, see <hi rendition="#italic">Documents</hi> (R.I.I.A.)
                    for 1952, pp. 116–162.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
